From 443123b905728b8d6aa79709f282a3c5c25815b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 07:53:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Implement import.meta, with url on it and resolve
 deliberately absent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`import.meta` was a SyntaxError — "import.meta not supported" — from the
compiler's meta-property path, which handled only new.target. Deterministic
rather than a crash, so it was carried as a capability decision rather than a
defect. This is the decision: import.meta is implemented, `url` is on it, and
`resolve` is not.

It compiles to a read of `meta` off the module record the body already receives
as its `module` parameter, so the object's identity, its lazy creation and
everything on it belong to the module host rather than to the compiler. That is
what keeps `import.meta === import.meta` true and lets a host with its own key
form report its own URL without the compiler knowing what a module key is.

JSModule.Meta creates the object once and then returns the same one, because a
module is entitled to hang state off it (ES2025 16.2.1.9). It has a null
prototype, so a property a module adds cannot be confused with one inherited
from Object.prototype, and it carries `url` and nothing else.

The URL comes from JSModuleContext.GetModuleUrl, a new virtual, because only
the host knows what its keys are. The default handles the two forms this
context produces: a key that is already an absolute URI is reported verbatim,
and a filesystem path becomes a file:// URL, since import.meta.url is specified
as a URL and a bare path is not one. Returning null is meaningful — the object
then carries no `url` at all rather than an invented one, so a module whose key
cannot be expressed as a URL reads undefined, which a script can detect.

resolve is absent, and the reason is this context's resolver rather than the
amount of code. Resolve is existence-based: it probes the filesystem and
answers null for a specifier that does not name a file that is there, while
import.meta.resolve is specified to resolve a specifier to a URL whether or not
anything is at it. Built on this resolver it would throw where a browser
answers — a wrong answer to a resolution question rather than a missing one,
and a page can feature-detect the absence but not the wrongness. Making the
resolver able to answer without loading is its own change.

The two error paths are separate on purpose. Outside module code import.meta
stays an early SyntaxError per ES2025 13.3.12 — a script, an ordinary eval, a
Function body — which is what a try { eval('import.meta') } feature-detect
expects. Inside module code compiled without a module record the construct is
legal but has nothing to read, so it is a deterministic ReferenceError naming
that rather than a silent undefined a page would then dereference.

Eight regressions in ImportMetaTests. Every expectation except resolve is
Chromium's measured answer for a module in a page, taken from the same probe:
identity stable, prototype null, Object.keys ['url'], url the module's own
absolute URL — a transitively imported module reports its own, not the entry
point's. Chromium also carries resolve; the last test pins its absence with the
reason.
